Subject: l10n: the composed locale decides the application locale on every platform

Patch 0114 closed the locale leak on Linux by pinning the browser's locale
environment. It closed Linux and only Linux, because the environment is the
Linux lever and only the Linux lever. macOS and Windows resolve the application
locale from somewhere the environment does not reach, and on macOS that is not
a regression waiting for a build -- it is live in the artifact shipping today.

MEASURED, on the shipped macos-arm64 bundle, system language varied per launch
through the app's own `AppleLanguages` default, composed Windows persona:

    system language      Intl    calendar   month     navigator.languages
    (host, English)      en-US   gregory    January   en-SG,en
    fr-FR                fr      gregory    janvier   en-GB,en
    th-TH                th      buddhist   มกราคม    en-SG,en
    ja-JP                ja      gregory    1月       en-SG,en
    de-DE                de      gregory    Januar    en-SG,en
    ar                   ar      gregory    يناير     en-US,en

Read the last two columns together. This is not one leak, it is a
*contradiction*: the persona announces English-Singapore language preferences
and then formats every date in Thai on a Buddhist calendar. Two unrelated
producers, one page, and a page needs a single `toLocaleString` call to see
them disagree. The Linux failure at least agreed with itself.

The same measurement on the launch contract's strongest locale layer:

    --fingerprint-locale=de-DE,de   navigator.languages de-DE,de
                                    Intl th, calendar buddhist, month มกราคม

So `--fingerprint-locale` was being honoured on one half of the surface and
ignored on the other. `--lang=de-DE` moved neither. `--fingerprint=host`
reported th throughout, which is correct and is the one arm that must not
change.

Also moving with the host's system language, and page-readable: `hourCycle`
(h12 for the English host and ar, h23 for fr/th/ja/de), the rendered width of
an `<input type=date>` (110.33px host, 109.53 fr, 106.33 th, 127.93 ja, 109.33
de), locale-dependent CJK font fallback (`measureText` 161.44 everywhere except
160.0 under ja-JP), `Intl.DisplayNames`, `RelativeTimeFormat` and number
grouping. All of it from one resolution, which is why one lever fixes all of it.

WHY MACOS LEAKS TODAY AND LINUX DID NOT

`scripts/package-artifact.sh` ships `Chromium.app` wholesale, and the locale
data is inside it. Not the 55 `.lproj` directories in `Contents/Resources` --
those hold `InfoPlist.strings` and nothing else -- but **220 `locale.pak` files
in `Chromium Framework.framework/Versions/<v>/Resources/<locale>.lproj/`, which
is where `ResourceBundle::GetLocaleFilePath` looks on Apple
(resource_bundle_mac.mm:68-84). That is the same full set the Linux build
produces and packaging used to discard, and it has always been there. So the
lever the pak fix turned on for Linux this morning has been on for macOS for
as long as the artifact has existed.

PER-PLATFORM RESOLUTION, AND A CORRECTION TO MY OWN 0114 REPORT

All three reach `SetICUDefaultLocale` through
`ResourceBundle::LoadLocaleResources` -> `l10n_util::GetApplicationLocale`,
whose default argument makes that call (resource_bundle.cc:507,
l10n_util.cc:392-399). What they resolve *from* differs:

  linux    `g_get_language_names()` -- glib's LANGUAGE / LC_ALL / LC_MESSAGES /
           LANG precedence (l10n_util.cc:344-358). `pref_locale` is never even
           pushed as a candidate. Pinned by 0114.

  macos    `GetLocaleOverride()` first, filled by
           `OverrideLocaleWithCocoaLocale()` from
           `OuterBundle().preferredLocalizations` in
           `ChromeMainDelegate::PreBrowserMain`, then `pref_locale`, then the
           literal "en-US". On macOS `pref_locale` is always empty in
           production -- `chrome_resource_bundle_helper.cc:74-78` passes `""`
           unless running tests -- so Cocoa decides, alone.

  windows  `pref_locale` FIRST, then `GetLocaleOverrides()`, then
           `base::i18n::GetConfiguredLocale()`.

The correction is Windows, and it is mine: 0114's report said Windows "falls
through to `GetUserDefaultLocaleName`". That is where the ICU fallback comes
from, but the fallback is not what answers. `content_main_runner_impl.cc:1210-1217`
calls `l10n_util::OverrideLocaleWithUILanguageList()` immediately after
`PreBrowserMain` whenever the override is empty, filling it from
`GetThreadPreferredUILanguageList()`. So in the browser the second candidate
always has a value and the ICU fallback is unreachable; it is reachable only in
a process where that call never ran, and a Windows child gets `--lang` from the
browser as `pref_locale` instead. The leak on Windows is the user's **preferred
UI language list**, not the user default locale. The fix is the same either way,
but the claim was wrong and is worth being right about.

Note also what both guards have in common: each is `if (override.empty())`,
with the comment "Don't do this if the locale is already set, which is done by
integration tests to ensure tests always run with the same locale." Chromium
already has a "the locale is pinned, leave it alone" concept on both platforms.

THE INTERVENTION

One hunk, in `GetApplicationLocaleInternal`, ahead of the platform dispatch:

    if (std::string composed = ApostateApplicationLocale(); !composed.empty())
      return composed;

That is the single point all three platforms share, and it has to be this one
rather than the two platform slots, for three reasons.

It is ahead of everything. Pre-filling `GetLocaleOverride()` on macOS would
work, but pre-filling `GetLocaleOverrides()` on Windows would not: `pref_locale`
is consulted first, so a `--lang` or -- worse -- a `kApplicationLocale` already
stored in an existing `--user-data-dir`'s Local State would outrank the composed
persona's locale, forever, through a persisted channel. Resolving above the
candidate list has no such hole.

It needs no ordering argument. The two platform slots would each need a startup
hook placed correctly relative to the compositor, which is the class of bug that
bit this codebase twice in one day (0084's `PreSandboxStartup`, 0110's move to
`BasicStartupComplete`). This runs *at* resolution time, in `BrowserMainLoop`,
which is unconditionally after the compositor at `PreSandboxStartup`. There is
nothing to get wrong.

And it is one decision with three readers. `ComposedApplicationLocale()` is new
in `base/apostate/compose.cc` and is now what 0114's environment write reads
too -- both of 0114's call sites were computing the answer inline and now call
the accessor instead. Two mechanisms that disagreed would be worse than either;
these cannot, because there is only one.

Precedence, unchanged from the launch contract and now identical on all three:

  explicit   `--fingerprint-locale`, where an operator's choice and the
             launchers' GeoIP answer both arrive.
  composed   the installed profile's own `locale.accept_languages` -- what an
             authored `--apostate-profile` carries -- then
             `kComposedDefaultLocale`, `en-US`.
  host       empty, meaning inherit, for exactly three launches:
             `--fingerprint=host`, a catalogue that cannot serve the launch, and
             any process of a browser that is not ours. All three install no
             profile, which is the whole of the condition.

Read off the command line, never through `Profile::Get()`: `Get()` caches on
first call, and locale resolution is not a place that can promise to run after
anything. The command line is also the one channel a child has, so browser and
child answer identically without consulting each other.

NO C-LIBRARY SPLIT, WHICH IS THE OBJECTION THIS HAD TO SURVIVE

`intl.resolved-locale` warns against forcing the UI locale while the C library
keeps the operator's value, because the split is observable through `strftime`
and `LANG`. It does not arise here.

On macOS -- which is POSIX, so `content_main.cc:255` did adopt the operator's
environment -- 0114 already rewrites the four variables and re-runs `setlocale`
from `"C"`. The C library is therefore already at the composed locale before
this resolves, and both halves derive from the same accessor. macOS needs both
patches and gets them.

On Windows nothing ever adopted the environment: the `setlocale(LC_ALL, "")`
block is `IS_POSIX && !IS_ANDROID`, and `base::win::SetupCRT`
(process_startup_helper.cc:50-59) only touches CRT report modes. The CRT stays
in the `"C"` locale exactly as on stock, so there is no adoption to contradict.
0114's environment write is inert on Windows and stays that way.

THE GATE, AND THE FAILURE THE WITHDRAWN PATCH HAD

The composed locale is run through `CheckAndResolveLocale` before it is
returned, so a locale this build ships no data for is not returned. That check
matters twice over. A locale with no bundle would otherwise produce an empty
application locale and kill the browser at
`chrome_resource_bundle_helper.cc:90`'s `CHECK`. And an unresolvable composed
locale must not fall through to the platform candidates, because that would
serve the host's locale under a composed persona -- the entire defect -- so it
falls back to `kComposedDefaultLocale` and logs, never to the host.

Verified rather than assumed, because assuming it is exactly how the earlier
attempt at this surface compiled clean and did nothing: on the shipped
macos-arm64 bundle `Chromium Framework.framework/.../en.lproj/locale.pak`
exists, one of 220, so `CheckAndResolveLocale("en-US")` succeeds there. On
linux-x64 the artifact now ships 440 paks including `en-US.pak`.

--LANG IS NOW INERT UNDER A COMPOSED PROFILE, AND SAYS SO

On Linux and macOS `--lang` already moved nothing, measured on both shipped
targets, and said nothing. On Windows it was the first candidate, so this makes
it inert there -- a real behaviour change, and the only one in this patch.

Reported rather than honoured or refused. Honouring it would let a UI language
contradict the persona, which is the pair of producers this surface exists to
keep together. Refusing would fail launches that pass `--lang` for reasons that
have nothing to do with the fingerprint. So `--fingerprint-explain` names it:
`ComposeRequest::accept_languages` is the switch's value, patch 0111 recorded
that nothing read it and filed the silence as its own defect, and this is that
defect closed -- the field now feeds a limitation when it disagrees with the
resolved application locale, and stays quiet when it agrees.

VERIFICATION

Compiled: `base/apostate/compose.cc`, `base/apostate/compose_unittest.cc`,
`ui/base/l10n/l10n_util.cc` and `ui/base/l10n/l10n_util_unittest.cc`, every
object, on the pinned macos-arm64 build.

Run here, on the pinned macos-arm64 build: `base_unittests
--gtest_filter='ApostateComposeTest.*'` -- 33 tests, including the two this
adds, which drive `ComposedApplicationLocale()` through the process command
line across all five precedence arms.

`ui_base_unittests --gtest_filter='L10nUtilTest.*'` -- 15 tests pass. Said
precisely, because the distinction matters: the new
`ApostateComposedLocaleOutranksThePlatformCandidates` is compiled OUT on this
host, so that run proves the suite is not broken and does not execute the new
test. It is excluded on Apple and Android for the reason Chromium's own
`GetAppLocale` test is -- on Apple `GetLocaleFilePath` resolves against the
framework bundle's `<locale>.lproj` and a test binary is not a bundle, so the
locale data cannot be faked through `ui::DIR_LOCALES`. It executes on the Linux
and Windows bots, which is where the candidate-order change lives.

So the Apple branch was executed separately, through a throwaway that pointed
`base::apple::SetOverrideFrameworkBundlePath` at the shipped artifact's
framework, so the real 220-pak bundle answered `CheckAndResolveLocale`. Removed
before this patch was generated; five arms, all with `pref_locale` = `fr` and
the platform candidate therefore servable:

    no --apostate-profile            -> fr       Chromium's own candidates, untouched
    composed, no locale named        -> en-US    the composed default wins
    --fingerprint-locale=de-DE,de    -> de       narrowed by CheckAndResolveLocale,
                                                 because the bundle ships de and
                                                 not de-DE. Chromium's behaviour,
                                                 and what the live artifact reports
    --fingerprint-locale=sw-KE,sw    -> sw       same narrowing; Swahili IS shipped,
                                                 so this resolves rather than
                                                 falling back. My own expectation
                                                 here was wrong and the code was
                                                 right, which is why the committed
                                                 test uses a tag no build ships
    --fingerprint-locale=tlh-AA,tlh  -> en-US    NOT fr. The safety property: an
                                                 unservable composed locale falls
                                                 back to the composed default and
                                                 never through to the host

That last line is the one this patch most needed to measure, and the fourth is
the reason the committed test does not use a plausible language: a real tag
region-strips to something a real build ships, and would have tested nothing.

Not verified: no linked browser has run this, because the next build has not
happened. Two lines are outside everything above -- that
`GetApplicationLocaleInternal` is the function the browser's own resolution
reaches, and that it reaches it after `InstallComposedProfile`. Both are
structural rather than conditional, and
`scripts/checks/release-smoke.mjs` against a fresh macos-arm64 artifact with
the host's system language set to something non-English is what confirms them;
docs/FINGERPRINTS.md §8 carries that command and its expected output.

Ledger: intl.resolved-locale, navigator.languages, http.accept-language,
coh.intl-locale-vs-navigator-language, coh.locale-language-agreement.

